WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.540
How can a cell be ready to destroy itself within hours, and still never do it by

2
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.780
mistake? The killer is an enzyme called a caspase.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.900
It cuts other proteins.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.140
Nothing undoes a cut.

5
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.160
So where can the cell place its safeguard?

6
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:27.763
Every caspase is made ahead of time, as an inactive precursor, so it is ready to

7
00:00:27.763 --> 00:00:33.943
act. An initiator, such as caspase-9, starts as a single inactive chain.

8
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:39.303
It turns on only when a platform gathers several copies, and they pair up.

9
00:00:40.103 --> 00:00:44.843
Then it cuts an executioner caspase, which is already paired but inactive.

10
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:49.823
So the first step is a gathering, and every step after it is a cut.

11
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:55.667
One part of the platform, APAF-1, sits in the cytosol.

12
00:00:56.487 --> 00:01:01.507
The other part, cytochrome c, is kept behind the outer membrane of the

13
00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:04.307
mitochondrion, so no platform forms.

14
00:01:05.087 --> 00:01:08.527
If that membrane opens, cytochrome c escapes.

15
00:01:09.787 --> 00:01:16.567
Cytochrome c and the nucleotide dATP then make seven copies of APAF-1 assemble

16
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:22.147
into a wheel. This wheel, the apoptosome, gathers caspase-9.

17
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:31.830
At that membrane, a family of proteins named for BCL-2 decides, and it has three

18
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:38.430
classes. BAX and BAK, the effectors, open the outer membrane once they are

19
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:44.310
activated. BCL-2 and its relatives, the guards, hold them in check.

20
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.010
The sensors, such as BAD and BID, carry one short helix.

21
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.910
It fits into a groove on a guard and occupies it.

22
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:59.683
In a healthy cell, guards and effectors are both present.

23
00:02:00.403 --> 00:02:03.543
Their proportion sets how hard the cell must be pushed.

24
00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:08.803
A stress must switch on the sensors, and they must outnumber the guards.

25
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:14.583
Then the guards are occupied, BAX and BAK are free, and the membrane opens.

26
00:02:15.603 --> 00:02:18.163
Cytochrome c escapes from the mitochondrion.

27
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:22.017
Now follow the whole chain, in order.

28
00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:27.577
The sensors outnumber the guards, and BAX and BAK open the membrane.

29
00:02:28.657 --> 00:02:33.497
Cytochrome c escapes, and the wheel assembles and gathers caspase-9.

30
00:02:34.417 --> 00:02:37.357
Then the cuts begin, one after another.

31
00:02:38.337 --> 00:02:44.017
Keep this rule: the balance of guards and sensors decides before the first cut,

32
00:02:44.437 --> 00:02:46.417
because nothing undoes a cut.
